  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nA stationary front will remain just south of Kansas through\nFriday. There is a very small chance of a t-storm this evening\nalong the front near the KS/OK state line and any storm that\nmanages to form could become severe with large hail. Later \ntonight some elevated convection may form across parts of \ncentral Kansas along a low to mid level baroclinic zone and then\nmove eastward. However, the latest model guidance favors\neastern Kansas. Chances for rain across southwest Kansas are \nvery low this evening and tonight (20% or less), with highest \nchances (30-40%) across central Kansas.\n\nUpper level ridging will expand across the plains through the\nweekend and then persist into early next week. This will lead \nto very hot weather, with highs from 100-105 degrees Saturday \nand 102 to 107 by Sunday, Monday and Tuesday. However, given \nthat the warm front will have shifted north of I-70, resulting \nin deep mixing and dilution of the moisture in the vertical, \nheat indices will stay a little below the actual temperature. \nNevertheless, heat headlines may be required as we get closer \nto the weekend.\n\nThe upper level ridge will begin to flatten out by mid next \nweek. The biggest forecast problem is the amplitude of an upper \nlevel trough progressing from southwest Canada into the Great \nLakes region that will push a cold front through Kansas. There \nis uncertainty among the various ensemble means and clusters \nabout the strength and timing of the front. Wednesday should be \ncooler along I-70; but 100 degree heat may persist along the \nKS/OK state line, especially if the front is slow. T-storms are\nalso a possibility by Wednesday as weak upper level impulses \nmove across the Rockies and approach the high plains along the \nsouthern extremity of the westerlies. High temperatures in the \n80s are strongly suggested by the ensemble means by Thursday, \nwith the Grand Ensemble showing 60-70% chances for .5\" or \ngreater of rain across central and western Kansas during the\nWednesday to Friday time frame. Keep in mind that the frontal \nlocation will make a large difference in rainfall patterns and \nthe highest chances of heavier rains could shift southward if \nthe frontal surge is stronger. The potential for locally heavier\nrain is expressed by the 75th/90th percentiles, which indicate\n1\" and even 2\" rains.",
